/**
	Whether the script set the voice of the voice, with set_voice or set_language, rather than the voice following the platform's own setting.
	bool tts_voice::voice_set;
	## Remarks:
		This property is read-only. Until the voice is set, the object speaks with the voice the platform's settings select, which it follows when the user changes them while the program runs: on Windows the default voice chosen in the system's speech settings; on Android the default voice, for the language chosen in the system's text-to-speech settings, of the engine preferred there, or, once the preferred engine's app is removed, of the highest ranked engine left, so changing the preferred engine, the language or the engine's own default voice changes it; on Apple platforms the voice chosen in the system's Spoken Content settings, or another voice of its language when that is a Siri voice, which apps can't use; on Linux the voice of Speech Dispatcher's configuration. It turns false again when the list is made again, by refresh, by set_language or by the object itself as the installed voices change (see refresh), and no longer holds the chosen voice, which was uninstalled for instance, or whose engine's app was removed on Android; the object then goes back to the voice the platform's settings select. It remembers the chosen voice, though: when a later list holds that voice again, as after an update that removed and installed it again, the object chooses it again and voice_set turns true, unless the script chose another voice or a language meanwhile (an empty language, which only lifts the filter, doesn't count). When a language filter set with set_language holds no voice but the builtin one once the chosen voice has left, the filter is lifted too, as set_language("") would, so that the platform's voice speaks; it isn't set again when the voice comes back.
*/

// Example:
void main() {
	tts_voice v;
	alert("Before", "voice_set: " + v.voice_set);
	v.set_voice(0);
	alert("After", "voice_set: " + v.voice_set);
}
